  "pages": [
    {
      "sections": [
        {
          "kind": "intro",
          "paragraphs": [
            "German signals mostly show **what to expect**: a main signal (Hauptsignal) says whether you may pass it and at what speed, a distant signal (Vorsignal) what the next main signal shows. Speeds on signals and boards are **number × 10 km/h**. Four signal families appear on the TSW 7 routes: **H/V** (West), **Ks** (new lines), **Hl** (former DR, e.g. Leipzig – Dresden) and semaphores. **PZB 90** watches every signal, **LZB** takes over on high-speed lines, and the **SiFa** checks that you are awake. Quadrant controls are from TheJAG's German profiles."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "systems",
          "title": "Safety systems",
          "cols": 4,
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "PZB 90",
              "full": "Punktförmige Zugbeeinflussung",
              "summary": "Track magnets at **1000 Hz** (distant signal), **500 Hz** (about 250 m before the main signal, not at every signal) and **2000 Hz** (main signal). Acknowledge, then brake within the speed curve or PZB brakes.",
              "where": "Every German route",
              "quadrant": "**ENG 2** Wachsam (acknowledge), **ENG 1** Frei (release), **round aux** Befehl 40; right aux square PZB on / off"
            },
            {
              "name": "LZB",
              "full": "Linienzugbeeinflussung",
              "summary": "Continuous cab signalling by cable loop: target speed and distance up to 13 km ahead on the cab display; lineside signals may be dark. Above 160 km/h only under LZB.",
              "where": "High-speed lines, e.g. Kassel – Würzburg, Nürnberg – Ingolstadt, Köln – Aachen, Leipzig – Dresden",
              "quadrant": "LZB orders and Ende: **ENG 1** (Frei); nothing to press when LZB takes over"
            },
            {
              "name": "SiFa",
              "full": "Sicherheitsfahrschaltung",
              "summary": "Driver vigilance: a lamp after about 30 s without a SiFa action, a buzzer 2.5 s later, an emergency brake 2.5 s after that.",
              "where": "Every German train",
              "quadrant": "**Gear lever** = SiFa reset; left aux square SiFa on / off"
            },
            {
              "name": "ETCS",
              "full": "European Train Control System",
              "summary": "Replacing PZB / LZB on new lines in the real world.",
              "where": "Not covered here; TSG's BR 101 Expert has an ETCS variant",
              "quadrant": ""
            }
          ]
        },
